WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.246
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book four.

2
00:00:05.546 --> 00:00:08.735
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.035 --> 00:00:16.610
Unit two: Financial Analysis. Today you will interpret financial statements using even though / despite contrasts.

4
00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:19.655
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:23.752
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.617 --> 00:00:25.792
Balance sheet.

7
00:00:26.783 --> 00:00:31.082
Balance sheet means a statement of what a company owns and owes at a point in time.

8
00:00:31.683 --> 00:00:34.379
The balance sheet revealed heavy short-term debt.

9
00:00:34.983 --> 00:00:37.624
Your turn. Say: Balance sheet.

10
00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:41.607
Balance sheet.

11
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:43.641
Cash flow.

12
00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:47.915
Cash flow means the movement of money into and out of a business.

13
00:00:48.517 --> 00:00:51.457
Despite rising sales, cash flow turned negative.

14
00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.540
Your turn. Say: Cash flow.

15
00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:58.633
Cash flow.

16
00:00:59.383 --> 00:01:00.233
Revenue.

17
00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:04.910
Revenue means the total income from sales before costs are deducted.

18
00:01:05.517 --> 00:01:07.783
Revenue doubled after the export deal.

19
00:01:08.383 --> 00:01:10.989
Your turn. Say: Revenue.

20
00:01:13.833 --> 00:01:14.614
Revenue.

21
00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:16.444
Profit margin.

22
00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:21.179
Profit margin means the percentage of revenue that remains as profit.

23
00:01:21.783 --> 00:01:25.246
Garment factories often operate on thin profit margins.

24
00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:28.642
Your turn. Say: Profit margin.

25
00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:32.640
Profit margin.

26
00:01:33.383 --> 00:01:34.686
Liability.

27
00:01:35.683 --> 00:01:38.948
Liability means a debt or financial obligation.

28
00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:42.536
Unpaid supplier invoices are a current liability.

29
00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:46.069
Your turn. Say: Liability.

30
00:01:48.933 --> 00:01:50.399
Liability.

31
00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:51.977
Asset.

32
00:01:52.983 --> 00:01:56.318
Asset means something of value that a company owns.

33
00:01:56.917 --> 00:01:59.822
The factory building is the firm's largest asset.

34
00:02:00.417 --> 00:02:02.663
Your turn. Say: Asset.

35
00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:06.536
Asset.

36
00:02:07.283 --> 00:02:08.482
Investment.

37
00:02:09.483 --> 00:02:13.608
Investment means money put into something to gain a future return.

38
00:02:14.217 --> 00:02:17.795
The investment in solar panels paid back within four years.

39
00:02:18.383 --> 00:02:21.129
Your turn. Say: Investment.

40
00:02:23.967 --> 00:02:25.084
Investment.

41
00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:26.967
Valuation.

42
00:02:27.983 --> 00:02:31.306
Valuation means an estimate of what a company is worth.

43
00:02:31.917 --> 00:02:35.414
The startup's valuation rose after its second funding round.

44
00:02:36.017 --> 00:02:38.750
Your turn. Say: Valuation.

45
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:42.880
Valuation.

46
00:02:43.667 --> 00:02:47.097
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:54.283
Visal, this rice exporter reported record profits, yet it nearly ran out of cash. How is that possible?

48
00:02:55.383 --> 00:03:04.909
Even though profits looked strong, its buyers were paying ninety days late. Profit is an accounting figure; cash is what pays salaries.

49
00:03:06.017 --> 00:03:08.933
So the income statement told only half the story?

50
00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:18.124
Precisely. Despite healthy revenue, the balance sheet showed rising liabilities, because the firm had borrowed to cover the gap.

51
00:03:19.217 --> 00:03:21.402
What should an analyst check first, then?

52
00:03:22.517 --> 00:03:33.133
Operating cash flow. Although margins matter, a firm can survive low profits for a while, but it cannot survive without cash.

53
00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.033
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:40.253
Grammar focus: Even though and despite contrasts.

55
00:03:40.553 --> 00:03:41.705
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:42.417 --> 00:03:45.635
Even though profits look strong, cash flow may be weak.

57
00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:50.454
Despite record revenue, the firm struggled to pay suppliers.

58
00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:54.859
Even though the margin is thin, the business is highly stable.

59
00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:05.860
'Even though' is followed by a full clause (subject + verb). 'Despite' and 'in spite of' are followed by a noun or -ing form: despite rising sales, despite selling more.

60
00:04:06.617 --> 00:04:10.836
Be careful! Don't say: Despite the sales rose, cash fell.

61
00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.229
Say: Although sales rose, cash fell.

62
00:04:15.529 --> 00:04:18.365
Despite + noun; although + clause.

63
00:04:19.167 --> 00:04:21.691
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:24.325
Reading Beyond the Bottom Line.

65
00:04:24.625 --> 00:04:32.546
Financial analysis involves interpreting three core statements: the income statement, the balance sheet and the cash-flow statement.

66
00:04:33.533 --> 00:04:40.514
Even though investors often focus on profit, experienced analysts treat cash flow as the more reliable sign of health.

67
00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:48.213
A company may report impressive revenue despite customers paying late, leaving it unable to meet its liabilities.

68
00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:55.785
Ratios such as profit margin and debt-to-assets help analysts compare firms of different sizes on equal terms.

69
00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:02.875
Ultimately, sound valuation depends on reading these statements together rather than trusting a single headline number.

70
00:05:03.967 --> 00:05:07.876
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:08.717 --> 00:05:11.181
blank rising sales, the shop closed.

72
00:05:11.481 --> 00:05:15.064
A, Even though. B, Despite. C, Although of.

73
00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:20.425
The answer is B: Despite.

74
00:05:21.583 --> 00:05:24.512
Something of value that a company owns is an blank.

75
00:05:24.812 --> 00:05:28.755
A, liability. B, asset. C, revenue.

76
00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:34.369
The answer is B: asset.

77
00:05:35.517 --> 00:05:38.259
The movement of money into and out of a business is blank.

78
00:05:38.559 --> 00:05:43.130
A, cash flow. B, valuation. C, profit margin.

79
00:05:46.667 --> 00:05:48.887
The answer is A: cash flow.

80
00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:51.690
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:51.990 --> 00:06:01.262
A, Even though the price rose, demand stayed high.. B, Despite the price rose, demand stayed high.. C, Even though of the price, demand stayed high..

82
00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:08.750
The answer is A: Even though the price rose, demand stayed high..

83
00:06:09.917 --> 00:06:14.703
According to the reading, experienced analysts treat blank as the more reliable sign of health.

84
00:06:15.003 --> 00:06:18.865
A, revenue. B, profit. C, cash flow.

85
00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:24.818
The answer is C: cash flow.

86
00:06:26.033 --> 00:06:29.440
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:30.283 --> 00:06:33.839
Why is cash flow sometimes more important than reported profit?

88
00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:52.162
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:52.462 --> 00:07:30.997
Cash flow is sometimes more important than profit because a business pays its bills with cash, not with accounting figures. Even though a company may record a sale today, the customer might not pay for months. Meanwhile, salaries, rent and suppliers must be paid on time. Many profitable small businesses in Cambodia have failed despite strong sales simply because they ran out of cash. Therefore, I think managers should monitor cash flow weekly, while profit shows whether the business model works in the long run.

90
00:07:31.297 --> 00:07:33.598
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:34.483 --> 00:07:39.342
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and even though and despite contrasts.

92
00:07:39.642 --> 00:07:45.045
Today's value is financial literacy. Understand money well enough to make wise decisions.

93
00:07:45.345 --> 00:07:47.951
See you in Unit three. Goodbye!

94
00:07:48.251 --> 00:07:50.880
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

